<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=principia-mathematica-newton">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philosophiæ Naturalis Principia Mathematica</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1687">1687</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Principia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philosophiæ Naturalis Principia Mathematica, usually called the Principia, is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-newton">Isaac Newton</a>'s treatise on mechanics, published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1687 with the support of Edmond Halley and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a>. It set out the three laws of motion and the law of universal gravitation and used them to explain the orbits of planets, the tides and the motion of comets. It became a foundation of classical physics.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/principia-mathematica-newton.jpg" alt="Philosophiæ Naturalis Principia Mathematica" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Zhaladshar at English Wikisource. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Newton%27s_Principia_title_page.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1687</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>England</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/encyclopedia/isaac-newton">Isaac Newton</a> · <a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Robert Hooke claimed priority for the inverse-square law of gravity, which Newton denied.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/heliocentrism">Heliocentrism<span>c. 270 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/calculus">Calculus<span>1665–1666</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/theory-of-relativity">Theory of relativity<span>1905</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=principia-mathematica-newton">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
